categories: [tool-demand, patch-tool, atomicity, invariants, minimal-diff, computed-indentation, schema-guard, round-trip-safety, precondition-cas, reference-audit, affordance-gap]
why_included: >
  What an agent needs to WRITE safely — the richest tool-obligation inventory in the set, every
  scenario stating its invariant (what must NOT change). Transferable demands: minimal-byte-range
  edits (comments, blank lines, prose, sibling formatting preserved); layout-idiom preservation
  (keep an attr sameline vs exploded to a block, per the author's choice); the TOOL not the agent
  computes indentation, read from the destination's actual siblings — and the honest admission
  that the no-sibling fallback "deserves a spec sentence somewhere: nothing ratified names the
  default unit"; schema-guard-BEFORE-write refusing an out-of-vocabulary value AND naming the
  vocabulary in the error ("conformance-before-write is the whole point of binding the schema");
  round-trip safety as an invariant (parse(serialize(v)) == v — quote "true  " so it doesn't
  re-parse boolean, escape a leading-pipe value so it doesn't re-parse a node); atomic multi-op
  patches with pre-state path resolution; and rename-with-reference-audit that runs BEFORE the
  write and lists cross-document escapes as unverifiable rather than silently ignoring them.
  Closes on a .gap (P7): prose edits are the ONLY write class that had to leave the tool for a
  span-splice that bypasses every guarantee — the strongest single demand for a co-designed
  text-addressing form.
