; 03-modifying.scenarios.udon — a day in the life, afternoon
; Edit journeys for the future udon patch tool: at-addressed, atomic,
; indentation-computing, schema-checked, round-trip-safe. Conventions:
; ../README.md. Path syntax provisional (see 01). Ops used here:
;   |patch :file <f>            — atomic container; all ops or none
;     |require :path :equals    — precondition (compare-and-set)
;     |set :path :value         — replace a value
;     |add-value :path :value   — append an assignment to a stacked attr
;     |insert :parent :position — add a child node (body in !:udon: raw)
;     |delete :path             — remove a node / assignment
; A patch touches the minimal byte range: comments, blank lines, prose,
; and sibling formatting are preserved untouched.

|feature[modifying].day-in-the-life
  :date <2026-07-21>
  :tool udon-patch
  :path-syntax provisional-2026-07
  :corpus ../corpus/

  Joseph's morning rulings arrive at 13:00; the afternoon is writes.
  Every scenario here states its invariant: what must NOT change.

  |scenario[single-set]
    :at <13:05> :agent lexicographer
    :file corpus/vivarium.lexicon.udon

    Joseph settled the regime-change carve. One attribute flips.

    |given the lexicon as shipped
    |when
      |patch :file corpus/vivarium.lexicon.udon
        |set :path "||term[regime-change]:status" :value settled
    |then
      |expect-fragment
        !:udon:
          |term[regime-change] :status settled
      |invariant the entry's body prose, the parenthetical crediting
        Joseph's flag, and every other line of the file are byte-identical
      |invariant the sameline position of :status (on the |term line, not
        exploded to a block line) is preserved — patches respect the
        author's layout idiom

  |scenario[stack-append-through-sugar]
    :at <13:15> :agent ordinator
    :file corpus/terrestris.ordinum.udon

    Reclassify promise[ore-and-soil] as .state.regime — the diff studied
    at 11:30, now performed.

    |given the ordinum as shipped, where the promise line reads
      \|promise[ore-and-soil].state
    |when
      |add-value :path "||promise[ore-and-soil]:'$traits'" :value regime
    |then
      |expect-fragment
        !:udon:
          |promise[ore-and-soil].state.regime
      The wire says "append a $traits assignment"; the serializer must
      know the assignment's canonical SURFACE is the identity sugar and
      extend `.state` to `.state.regime` — not emit a legal-but-alien
      block line `:'$traits' regime`. Sugar-aware rendering is a real
      tool obligation the packet has not named: the wire model and the
      surface idiom diverge exactly at the $-designated attributes.

  |scenario[insert-with-computed-indentation]
    :at <13:30> :agent cartographer
    :file corpus/asf-processes.process-map.udon

    A new process lands in cluster[coherence]. The tool, not the agent,
    computes every column.

    |given the map as shipped
    |when
      |insert :parent "||cluster[coherence]" :position last
        !:udon:
          |process[theory-news-monitor]?
            :name Theory-news monitoring
            :health absent
            :drain healing
            :design-unknown the monitoring cadence + source list

            Watch the field for results that bear on the theory; route
            hits into finding-routing.
    |then the inserted block adopts the SIBLING column (|process at
      column 4), attrs at 6, prose at 6 — read from the destination's
      actual siblings, never assumed from a house default
      |invariant a blank line separates it from |process[drift-repair],
        matching the file's existing sibling rhythm
    |when the ordinator inserts a |predicate under
      promise[ore-and-soil], which has prose but NO element children
    |then the tool derives the child column from the parent's
      content-base (prose base = predicate base), and appends after the
      existing prose — with no sibling to copy, the computation falls
      back to parent column + the file's observed indent unit (2 here),
      and THAT fallback deserves a spec sentence somewhere: nothing
      ratified names the default unit

  |scenario[schema-guard-before-write]
    :at <13:50> :agent operator-2
    :file corpus/operata-live.workspace.udon

    |given the workspace as shipped, with operata.domain.udon bound as
      its schema
    |when
      |patch :file corpus/operata-live.workspace.udon
        |set :path "||intent[312]:status" :value resurrected
    |then
      |expect-error :code SchemaViolation
      The domain declares :one-of [projected active realized abandoned];
      the patch is refused BEFORE the file is touched, and the error
      names the vocabulary. Conformance-before-write is the whole point
      of binding the schema: an agent must not be able to write a state
      the consuming Ruby will choke on.
    |when
      |set :path "||intent[312]:claimed-by" :value 42
    |then
      |expect-error :code SchemaViolation :note claimed-by is .string;
        the integer 42 is refused, not coerced

  |scenario[write-safety-is-type-safety]
    :at <14:05> :agent lexicographer
    :file corpus/vivarium.lexicon.udon

    The serializer owns the boundary rules. Values that LOOK like other
    kinds must survive the round trip.

    |given the lexicon as shipped
    |when the agent sets a new :gloss attribute on term[memo] to the
      six-character STRING "true  " — trailing spaces included — and
      a :note whose text begins with a pipe
      ; (that line wrap is deliberate: a reflow that leaves ":note" line-
      ;  initial inside prose draws AttributeAfterChildren — the exact
      ;  sigil-promotion hazard CONSUMERS.md caught in the wild; a
      ;  formatting tool must wrap prose with the same awareness)
    |then the serializer must emit `:gloss "true  "` (quoted — a bare
      true would re-parse as a boolean and the spaces would vanish) and
      `:note \|looks structural...` or a quoted equivalent (a bare
      leading pipe in value position would re-parse as a NODE value)
      |invariant parse(serialize(v)) == v for every value written today —
        the patch tool's write path must consult the same scan rules the
        parser reads by (keywords alone at a boundary, sigil-leading
        blobs, framed ` ; ` in blobs, digit-leading strings like "0042")

  |scenario[atomic-claim]
    :at <14:20> :agent operator-1
    :file corpus/operata-live.workspace.udon

    The claim ritual from the domain's |update[claim], expressed as one
    atomic patch.

    |given the workspace as shipped, where intent 312 is unclaimed
    |when
      |patch :file corpus/operata-live.workspace.udon
        |require :path "||intent[312]:claimed-by" :equals nil
        |set :path "||intent[312]:claimed-by" :value operator-1
        |set :path "||intent[312]:claimed-at" :value <2026-07-21T14:20>
        |set :path "||intent[312]:status" :value active
    |then all four ops apply in one write, or none do
      |expect :applied 4
      |invariant no intermediate state is ever on disk (claimed with no
        claimed-at is the exact half-write the domain forbids)
      All :path values in one patch resolve against the PRE-patch tree —
      op 3 must not be re-addressed against a half-applied document.
      The packet is silent on patch-internal addressing semantics;
      pre-state resolution is what these scenarios assume throughout.

  |scenario[rename-with-reference-audit]
    :at <14:40> :agent cartographer
    :file corpus/asf-processes.process-map.udon

    Joseph renames the valve. Keys are referenced material.

    |given the map as shipped
    |when
      |patch :file corpus/asf-processes.process-map.udon
        |set :path "||process[valve]:'$key'" :value joseph-valve
    |then the tool reports every in-document reference to the old
      identity BEFORE applying
      !:text:
        renaming |process[valve] -> |process[joseph-valve]
        in-document references to update:
          |process[decision-surfacing]:feeds   @process[valve]
          |process[finding-routing]:feeds      @process[valve]
        out-of-scope (document-scoped tool cannot verify):
          corpus/archema.concept-matrix.udon   |carrier :ref @process[valve]
          corpus/vivarium.decision-log.udon    prose mention
      |expect :cascade offered :dangling-check in-document
      Whether the cascade auto-applies or the agent confirms is a tool
      policy; that the audit RUNS before the write is the contract. The
      cross-document escapes are listed as unverifiable, not silently
      ignored — the seam from 01's cross-document scenario again.

  |scenario[prose-edit-falls-back-to-api].gap
    :at <15:10> :agent lexicographer
    :file corpus/vivarium.lexicon.udon

    A typo in the memo entry's prose ("qeury-graph"). The trio's third
    member — modify — finally hits P7 for real.

    |given the lexicon as shipped
    |when the agent tries to patch the second prose paragraph of
      term[memo]
    |then no path addresses prose, so no |set can carry the edit; the
      journey drops to the API (parent node, text children by index) and
      a span-based splice
    |gap
      :packet-ref P7
      :shape a text-segment addressing form co-designed with the patch
        syntax — the read side never needed it (01 agreed), but the
        WRITE side has no principled fallback: span-splices bypass every
        guarantee (schema check, round-trip safety) the patch tool
        otherwise gives
      P7's "revisit together with patch syntax" is exactly right, and
      today supplies the evidence: prose edits are the only write class
      that had to leave the tool.
